   250	/// The FreeBSD implementation of `decommit`. It maps fresh memory over
   251	/// `memory` at the same address.
   252	///
   253	/// Returns true when the old pages have been released and the range reads
   254	/// as zeros. Returns false when nothing changed and the original contents
   255	/// are still in place.
   256	///
   257	/// FreeBSD's `madvise` cannot do this job. Both `MADV_DONTNEED` and
   258	/// `MADV_FREE` only mark pages as unused. The pages stay in memory, so the
   259	/// process's memory use does not go down, and the old bytes can still be
   260	/// read until the kernel needs that memory for something else.
   261	///
   262	/// Mapping new memory over the range with `MAP_FIXED` does work. The kernel
   263	/// removes the old mapping and adds the new one while holding the process's
   264	/// memory map lock, so no other thread sees the range in between. Terminal
   265	/// pages are private to this process, so removing the old mapping frees
   266	/// their physical memory immediately. The new mapping starts with no
   267	/// physical memory at all. The kernel supplies a zero-filled page the first
   268	/// time each page is touched. The kernel code this relies on
   269	/// (`vm_map_fixed`) behaves the same from FreeBSD 13.0 through 15.1.
   270	///
   271	/// One failure needs extra care. The kernel can remove the old mapping and
   272	/// then fail to add the new one, which leaves nothing mapped at the address.
